comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-29T16:56:18.081Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence that the transition half of this policy engine has no substrate at all today, measured on a sandbox tracker at build 2026.7.29.\n\nWhat is declared. The workspace schema file schema/workflows.json contains exactly six entries, and all six are role-to-status-name aliases: draft_status, open_status, in_progress_status, blocked_status, close_status, canceled_status. There is no transition list, no guard, no precondition and no required-field rule anywhere in schema data. So the lifecycle state machine exists only as control flow inside the mutation paths, and nothing publishes it.\n\nWhat that permits. A closed item moved to in_progress through update --status succeeds, exit 0, changed_field_count 4. A closed item moved to draft succeeds - into a tier the corpus has never once written, 0 of 40,856 entries. Closing an already-closed item succeeds and overwrites the stored close reason. None of the three emits a warning.\n\nWhy this matters beyond tidiness. Custom statuses can be registered with a lifecycle role, so a domain pack can add states; it cannot say which states may follow which, who may perform the move, or what must be supplied. That makes the universal-customization claim thin in exactly the place a non-software domain needs it most: a review state that anyone can leave, an approval that requires no approver. It also means an agent - or a reinforcement-learning policy treating the mutation surface as an action space - has no machine-readable way to enumerate the legal actions for an item in a given state, so legality can only be discovered by attempting a mutation and reading the outcome.\n\nScope note. The specific reopen transition and the clearing of terminal fields belong to pm-83cz0o. What is recorded here is the absence of a declared transition relation for this engine to enforce.\n"
body: ""
